From 5b7b8df0837cc54c8f10f5580fe0b4f4d0286922 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Roman=20Fro=C5=82ow?= <rofrol@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 05:28:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 43/44] Windows: RDTSCP and RDPID under Wine on macOS through
 TPIDR_EL0

Under Wine FEX has no processor-number register, so RDTSCP and RDPID are not
advertised and raise #UD (Blender 5.3.0 stops on it). macOS keeps the current
processor number in the low 12 bits of TPIDR_EL0, the value libsystem's
pthread_cpu_number_np returns; Apple does not document the layout.

- HostFeatures: the SupportsCPUIndexInTPIDRRO bit becomes CPUIndexSource
  (None, TPIDRRO, TPIDR_Low12), so two sources cannot both be set.
- JIT ProcessorID and CPUID's APIC ID read TPIDR_EL0 & 0xfff for TPIDR_Low12.
- New option CPUIndexInTPIDR (default off). When set under Wine, the unix
  library probes the layout on threads of both QoS classes against
  pthread_cpu_number_np and hw.ncpu, and the PE side against
  GetCurrentProcessorNumber(); any failure leaves the feature off. Up to 1
  mismatch in 1000 samples per thread is taken as a migration away and back
  between the reads.

Limits: a startup check cannot see aliasing above 4096 processors or a later
change; a thread that restricted its own affinity gets the processor it runs
on, while Wine's GetCurrentProcessorNumber names one inside its mask.
